  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1144 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nWater vapor and upper level analysis depicts a low centered over \nWyoming, a ridge extending up the West Coast, and another low just \noff-shore of California. At the surface, the low is over the western \nNebraska Panhandle with a weak trough extending northwestward along \nthe Bighorns. Latest radar imagery has a large swath of rain over \nthe western and central South Dakota plains, rotating counter-\nclockwise around the upper low. \n\nFor the rest of today, the main band of rain over the South Dakota \nplains will continue to lift northward, being replaced by a few \nsmaller, less organized showers for the afternoon and evening hours. \nRain showers across northeastern Wyoming will be less, as drier gets \npulled up from southeastern Wyoming. High temperatures will remain \ncool with the damp conditions and abundant cloud cover, with no \ndeviation from blended guidance which is close to current temps. \n\nThe upper low opens up and drifts east overnight, which will in-turn \nends the rain showers from west to east. Lingering moisture in the \nair, combined with surface pressure rises and light winds, will \nresult in areas of fog and low clouds into Monday morning. \nVisibility will gradually improve late Monday morning, with the \nlower ceilings and clouds sticking around throughout the day. \n\nMore clouds and some fog for MOnday night/Tuesday across the South \nDakota Plains and the southern Black Hills - with the Northern Black \nHills and portions of northeastern Wyoming becoming sunny. Clouds \nwill gradually decrease across the entire area through the day, with \nincreasing south-southeasterly winds. The strongest winds, 15 to 25 \nmph, will likely be across northwestern South Dakota where the \npressure gradient looks to be the tightest. \n\nA weak shortwave trough moves through Wednesday and Wednesday night, \nwhich could result in some showers across the area. The wave moves \nthrough fairly quickly, thus, some areas should remain dry. \n\nA drier, warmer pattern then sets up for the end of the week into \nthe weekend, with highs about 5 to 10 degrees above average.",
